# **NASA Space Apps Challenge 2026 — Met Office, Exeter**
*14th – 15th November 2026*

Our team (Damian, Freddie, James and Benjy), took on the Space Mission Design Game Challenge. Our aim was to design a game where the player targets their space craft towards an astronomical body and tries to successfully land.

I worked on many things, including designing the main startscreen from scratch using Pygame in VS Code. This file is attached as "main.py" inside the repository.
We also had to break down complex ideas, use real-life physics within the simulation game, and work to turn it into a presentable challenge, within a 3-minute slot, as well as answer questions about the project from the many judges.

The final prototype was many things merged together within Unity, and python code quickly translated into C#. This allowed us to have high-quality 3D physics, which greatly enhanced the simulation. The Unity aspect was a massive part of the overall project and unfortunaly is not within the repo.
As well as being a "simulation", we gamified it with Spacecoin, the in-game currency that you can get based on how succesful your launches are (depending on the conditions you enter, e.g 7500kg mass and 25000kN thrust). The ratio is 5 Spacecoin for every 10,000km succesfully travelled and huge bonuses for successful landings.
It also costs Spacecoin to launch your rockets, to further resemble real-life budget constraints. Move detail about this and how we presented it is available in the powerpoint attached within the repository.

Personally, one of the best things was working as part of a team, face to face, and solving problems together. This experience was invaluable and taught me a lot about how programmers work in the real world.

What we would have to do next: 
Integrate all aspects of the game into one, finished, polished product
Make the "Angle" of the rocket being launched more accurate, not just a singular vector but a more complex, accurate solution
Add many more destinations to make the game engaging and lasting, as so far we only have a singular "route" of launch, which is from Earth to the moon.

Huge thanks to the amazing team, it was an incredible event and I enjoyed the opportunity very much :) 
